   130	\begin{abstract}
   131	An integer-valued polynomial $P(x,y,z)$ is said to be universal (over $\Z$) if each nonnegative integer can be written as $P(x,y,z)$ with $x,y,z\in\Z$. In this paper, we mainly introduce a new technique to determine the universality of some sums in the form $x(a_1x+a_2)/2+y(b_1y+b_2)/2+z(c_1z+c_2)/2$
   132	(with $a_1-a_2,b_1-b_2,c_1-c_2$ all even)
   133	conjectured by Sun,  using various identities of Ramanujan's theta functions.
   134	For example, we prove that $x(3x+1)+y(3y+2)+2z(3z+2)$
   135	and $x(4x+r)+y(3y+1)/2+z(7z+1)/2\ (r=1,3)$ are universal.
   136	\end{abstract}
