   141	\section{Introduction}\label{Introduction}
   142	
   143	Let $\N=\{0,1,2,\ldots\}$ and $\Z^+=\{1,2,3,\ldots\}$.
   144	For an integer-valued polynomial $P(x_1,\ldots,x_n)$, we call it {\it universal} (over $\Z$) if
   145	$$\{P(x_1,\ldots,x_n):\ x_1,\ldots,x_n\in\Z\}=\N,$$
   146	and call $P(x_1,\ldots,x_n)$ {\it universal over $\N$} if
   147	$$\{P(x_1,\ldots,x_n):\ x_1,\ldots,x_n\in\N\}=\N.$$
   148	When
   149	$$\N\sm\{P(x_1,\ldots,x_n):\ x_1,\ldots,x_n\in\Z\}$$
   150	is finite, we say that $P(x_1,\ldots,x_n)$ is {\it almost universal} (over $\Z$).
   151	
   152	For each integer $m\gs3$, those numbers
   153	$$p_m(n)=(m-2)\bi n2+n=\f{(m-2)n^2-(m-4)n}2\ (n=0,1,2,\ldots)$$
   154	are called {\it $m$-gonal numbers}, and those numbers $p_m(x)$ with $x\in\Z$
   155	are usually called {\it generalized $m$-gonal numbers}. Note that
   156	\begin{equation}\label{p-}p_m(-x)=\f{x((m-2)x+m-4)}2.
   157	\end{equation}
   158	In particular,
   159	\begin{gather*} p_3(x)=\f{x(x+1)}2,\ p_3(-x)=\f{x(x-1)}2,\ p_4(x)= p_4(-x)=x^2,\ \\p_5(x)=\f{x(3x-1)}2,
   160	\ p_5(-x)=\f{x(3x+1)}2,
   161	\\p_6(x)=x(2x-1)=p_3(2x-1),\ p_6(-x)=x(2x+1)=p_3(2x),
   162	\\ p_7(x)=\f{x(5x-3)}2,\ p_7(-x)=\f{x(5x+3)}2,\ p_8(x)=x(3x-2),\ p_8(-x)=x(3x+2).
   163	\end{gather*}
   164	Obviously,
   165	\begin{equation}\label{p6}\{p_6(x):\ x\in\Z\}=\{x(2x+1):\ x\in\Z\}=\{p_3(x):\ x\in\Z\}=\{p_3(n):\ n\in\N\}.
   166	\end{equation}
   167	Fermat claimed that $p_m(x_1)+\cdots+p_m(x_m)$ is universal over $\N$. This was confirmed by
   168	Lagrange, Gauss and Cauchy for the cases $m=4$, $m=3$, and $m\gs5$, respectively.
   169	In 1862 Liouville (cf. \cite[p. 23]{Dickson}) determined all universal sums $ap_3(x)+bp_3(y)+cp_3(z)$ with $a,b,c\in\Z^+$,
   170	namely, those triples $(a,b,c)$ with $a\ls b\ls c$ such that $ap_3(x)+bp_3(y)+cp_3(z)$ is universal are
   171	$$(1,1,1),\ (1,1,2),\ (1,1,4),\ (1,1,5),\ (1,2,2),\ (1,2,3),\ (1,2,4).$$
   172	 In $2007$, Z.-W. Sun \cite{Sun} initiated the study of universal sums of the types
   173	 $ax^2+by^2+cp_3(z)$ and $ax^2+bp_3(y)+cp_3(z)$ with $a,b,c\in\Z^+$, and the investigation was finished
   174	 via the three papers \cite{Sun,GPS,OS}.
   175	
   176	 In 2010 B. Kane and Sun \cite{KaneSun} used modular forms and the theory of ternary quadratic forms to characterize those tuples $(a,b,c)\in(\Z^+)^3$
   177	 such that $ax^2+by^2+cp_3(z)$ is almost universal; they also investigated those tuples $(a,b,c)\in(\Z^+)^3$  such that $ax^2+bp_3(y)+cp_3(z)$ is almost universal, as well as those tuples $(a,b,c)\in(\Z^+)^3$  such that $ap_3(x)+bp_3(y)+cp_3(z)$ is almost universal.
   178	 Via solving a conjecture of Kane and Sun~\cite{KaneSun}, W. K. Chan and B.-K. Oh \cite{ChanOh} gave a complete characterization of those triples $(a,b,c)\in(\Z^+)^3$ with $ax^2+bp_3(y)+cp_3(z)$
   179	 (or $ap_3(x)+bp_3(y)+cp_3(z)$) almost universal.
   180	
   181	 In 2015 Sun \cite{Sun2015} investigated universal sums of the type $ap_i(x)+bp_j(y)+cp_k(z)$ with $a,b,c\in\Z^+$ and $i,j,k\in\{3,4,5,\ldots\}$. In particular,  for $k\in\{4,5,7,8,9,\ldots\}$ and $a,b,c\in\Z^+$ with $a\ls b\ls c$, Sun \cite{Sun2015} showed that if $ap_k(x)+bp_k(y)+cp_k(z)$ is universal then $k=5$, $a=1$  and $(b,c)$ is among the following $20$ ordered pairs:
   182	 $$(1,c)\, (c\in\{1,\ldots,10\}\sm\{7\}),\ (2,c)\, (c\in\{2,3,4,6,8\}),
   183	(3,c)\, (c=3,4,6,7,8,9).$$
   184	For these ordered pairs $(b,c)$, it is now known (cf. \cite{Guy,Sun2015,O}) that $p_5(x)+bp_5(y)+cp_5(z)$ is indeed universal.
   185	
   186	In $2017$,  Sun \cite{Sun1} proved that, for  integers $0 \ls b \ls c \ls d \ls a$ with $a >2$, the sum $x(ax +b) +y(ay+c) +z(az+d)$ is universal if and only if the quadruple $(a, b, c, d)$ is among
   187	$$(3, 0, 1, 2),~ (3, 1, 1, 2),~ (3, 1, 2, 2),~ (3, 1, 2, 3),~ (4, 1, 2, 3).$$
   188	Sun \cite{Sun1} also proved that $x(ax+1)+y(by +1)+z(cz +1)$ is universal if $(a, b, c)$ is among the following seven triples:
   189	$$(1, 2, 3),~ (1, 2, 4),~ (1, 2, 5),~ (2, 2, 4),~ (2, 2, 5),~ (2, 3, 3),~ (2, 3, 4).$$
   190	J. Ju and Oh \cite{JuOh} showed that $x(ax + 1) + y(by + 1) + z(cz + 1)$ is also
   191	universal for $(a, b, c) = (2, 2, 6),~ (2, 3, 5),~ (2, 3, 7)$ as conjectured
   192	by Sun \cite{Sun1}.
   193	
   194	For convenience, for any $a_1,b_1,c_1\in\Z^+$ and $a_2,b_2,c_2\in\Z$ with $a_1-a_2,b_1-b_2,c_1-c_2$
   195	 all even, whenever
   196	 \begin{equation}\label{xyz}\f{x(a_1x+a_2)}2+\f{y(b_1y+b_2)}2+\f{z(c_1z+c_2)}2
   197	\end{equation}
   198	is universal (resp., almost universal), we call the tuple $(a_1,a_2,b_1,b_2,c_1,c_2)$  universal
   199	(resp., almost universal).
   200	For any $a\in\Z^+$, by \eqref{p6} we have
   201	$$\{ap_3(x):\ x\in\Z\}=\l\{\f{x(ax+a)}2:\ x\in\Z\r\}=\left\{\f{x(4ax+2a)}2:\ x\in\Z\right\}.$$
   202	Sun \cite{Sun2,Sun3} investigated those {\it standard} universal tuples $(a_1,a_2,b_1,b_2,c_1,c_2)\in\N^6$
   203	with $a_1-a_2, b_1-b_2,c_1-c_2\in2\Z^+$, $a_1\gs b_1\gs c_1\gs2$,
   204	and $a_2\gs b_2$ if $a_1=b_1$, and $b_2\gs c_2$ if $b_1=c_1$; he
   205	showed that such tuples $(a_1,a_2,b_1,b_2,c_1,c_2)$ are among the $12082$ tuples listed in \cite[Appendix]{Sun2}.
   206	 Those tuples in the list marked with $*$ have been proved to be universal,
   207	 and Sun \cite{Sun2,Sun3} conjectured that all the remaining tuples in the list are indeed universal.
   208	 In this direction, H.-L. Wu and Sun \cite{WuSun} made certain progress.
   209	 As Sun \cite{Sun1} proved that $x(2x+1)+y(3y+1)+z(4z+1)$ is universal, the tuple $(8,2,6,2,4,2)$
   210	 not marked with $*$ in the list is indeed universal. Note that all previous works in this direction
   211	 depend heavily on the theory of ternary quadratic forms.
   212	
   213	 In Section 2, we will confirm that some open tuples listed in \cite[Appendix]{Sun2} are indeed universal
   214	 by using the theory of ternary quadratic forms.
   215	
   216	Ramanujan's general theta function is defined by
   217	\begin{equation}\label{2.1}f(a,b):= \sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty}{{a}^{n(n+1)/2}}{{b}^{n(n-1)/2}}\ \ \ \t{with}\ |ab|<1.\end{equation}
   218	In particular, Ramanujan \cite[Entry 22]{Adiga3} introduced $\varphi(q)$ and $\psi(q)$  for $|q|<1$:
   219	\begin{align}\label{2.7}\varphi(q)&:= f(q,q) =  \sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty} q^{n^2},\\
   220	\label{2.8}\psi(q)&:= f(q,q^3) =  \sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty} {q^{n(2n+1)}}=  \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} {q^{n(n+1)/2}}.
   221	\end{align}
   222	For various formulas of Ramanujan's theta functions, one may consult the excellent introductory book
   223	of B.C. Berndt \cite[Chapter 1]{Berndt}.
   224	
   225	In this paper we find that many universal sums can be studied via identities of Ramujan's theta functions.
   226	We establish the following novel theorem connecting universal sums with Ramujan's theta functions.
   227	
   228	\begin{theorem}\label{Th1.1} Let $i,j,s,t,u,v\in\Z$ with $i+j,s+t,u+v$ positive.
   229	Suppose that
   230	\begin{equation}\label{=}f(q^i,q^j)f(q^s,q^t)f(q^u,q^v)=\sum_{r=1}^k m_rq^{r-1}f(q^{ka_{1r}},q^{ka_{2r}})
   231	f(q^{kb_{1r}},q^{kb_{2r}})f(q^{kc_{1r}},q^{kc_{2r}}),
   232	\end{equation}
   233	where $a_{1r},a_{2r},b_{1r},b_{2r},c_{1r},c_{2r}\in\Z$ and $k,m_r,a_{1r}+a_{2r},b_{1r}+b_{2r},c_{1r}+c_{2r}\in\Z^+$.
   234	
   235	{\rm (i)} The tuple $(i+j,i-j,s+t,s-t,u+v,u-v)$ is universal if and only if
   236	$$(a_{1r}+a_{2r},a_{1r}-a_{2r},b_{1r}+b_{2r},b_{1r}-b_{2r},c_{1r}+c_{2r},c_{1r}-c_{2r})$$
   237	is universal for all $r=1,\ldots,k$.
   238	
   239	{\rm (ii)} The tuple $(i+j,i-j,s+t,s-t,u+v,u-v)$ is almost universal if and only if the tuple
   240	$$(a_{1r}+a_{2r},a_{1r}-a_{2r},b_{1r}+b_{2r},b_{1r}-b_{2r},c_{1r}+c_{2r},c_{1r}-c_{2r})$$
   241	is almost universal for all $r=1,\ldots,k$.
   242	\end{theorem}
   243	
   244	Utilizing the above theorem and some known identities on the theta functions, we establish the following
   245	theorems whose results were conjectured by Sun \cite{Sun2}.
   246	
