   908	
   909	\medskip
   910	\noindent{\tt Proof of Theorem \ref{Th1.4}}.
   911	(i) Kane and Sun \cite{KaneSun} showed that the sum
   912	 $$x^2+\frac{y(y+1)}{2}+9\frac{z(z+1)}{2}$$
   913	 is almost universal. Note that
   914	   $$x^2+\frac{y(y+1)}{2}+9\frac{z(z+1)}{2} \sim 9 x(2x+1)+y(3y+1)+\frac{z(3z+1)}{2} $$
   915	   by \eqref{SunLemma1} and \eqref{p6}. So the tuple $(36,18,6,2,3,1)$ is almost universal.
   916	
   917	    Let $|q|<1$. Multiplying both sides of \eqref{aAthm13u1}
   918	by $\psi(q^9)=f(q^9,q^{27})$, we find that
